```

Two consumers subscribe to these events at module load: `activity-service.ts::registerActivityListeners()` (writes audit rows — you'd add a case here for a new `member.ownership_*` action, plus add the action string to `ActivityAction` in `src/types/activity.ts`) and `notification-service.ts`'s `registerFanOut()` (writes notification rows — see #8 below).

## 6. Closest existing analog: **invitations** (`src/server/services/invitation-service.ts`, `src/server/repositories/invitation-repository.ts`, `src/schemas/invitation.ts`, `src/server/db/schema/members.ts` — `invitations` table)

This is exactly the pattern to clone for a time-limited handover offer:

- **Type** (`src/types/member.ts`):
```ts
export interface Invitation extends Timestamps, TenantScoped {
  readonly id: InvitationId;
  readonly email: string;
  readonly role: Role;
  readonly invitedBy: UserId;
  readonly token: string;
  readonly expiresAt: IsoTimestamp;
  readonly acceptedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
  readonly revokedAt: IsoTimestamp | null;
}
```
- **Schema constants**: `DEFAULT_EXPIRY_DAYS = 14` in invitation-service.ts (you'd use `7` for handover); token minted via `randomToken(32)` (`src/lib/hash.ts`, `crypto.randomBytes(32).toString("base64url")`), stored hashed via `hashToken(token)` (SHA-256 hex), looked up later by `findInvitationByTokenHash(hashToken(token))` — **never stores the raw token**.
- **Expiry math** happens in the repository at insert time:
```ts
const expiresAt = toIsoTimestamp(new Date(Date.now() + input.expiresInDays * MS_PER_DAY));
```
- **Accept flow runs unauthenticated** (`acceptInvitation(userId, input)` — no `Actor`, because the hashed token itself is the credential/tenant-carrier). Checks are inline, throwing plain `Error`s, no typed class:
```ts
if (invitation.revokedAt !== null) throw new Error("That invitation has been revoked");
if (invitation.acceptedAt !== null) throw new Error("That invitation has already been accepted");
if (invitation.expiresAt <= toIsoTimestamp(new Date())) throw new Error("That invitation has expired");
```
This is your model for "pending" vs "accepted" vs "revoked" vs "expired" — there is **no separate `status` enum column**; state is inferred from three nullable timestamp columns (`acceptedAt`, `revokedAt`, plus comparing `expiresAt` against now). Follow this same nullable-timestamp modeling for the handover offer table rather than adding a `status` text enum, to match the existing convention.
- **No background "lapse" job for invitations** — expiry is checked lazily at accept time only (there's no cron job that flips expired invites to some other state). If you want proactive lapsing you'd be introducing a new pattern; check `src/server/jobs/` (e.g. `trial-expiry-job.ts`) for the nearest analog to a scheduled expiry sweep.
- **DB schema** (`src/server/db/schema/members.ts`), `invitations` table — copy this shape for a `ownership_handovers` table (tenant columns + timestamps, `tokenHash` unique index, no soft-delete columns since invitations aren't soft-deleted).
- Revoke/resend pattern: `revokeInvitation` sets `revokedAt`; `resendInvitation` revokes the old row and mints a brand new one rather than mutating expiry — a "re-offer handover" flow should probably do the same.

## 7. Database layer

**SQL via Drizzle ORM + better-sqlite3**, not in-memory. `drizzle.config.ts`:
```ts
export default {
  schema: "./src/server/db/schema/index.ts",
  out: "./drizzle",
  dialect: "sqlite",
  dbCredentials: { url: "./data/taskflow.db" },
  strict: true, verbose: true,
} satisfies Config;
```
**Important**: there is **no `./drizzle` migrations directory checked into the repo** (confirmed — no `.sql` files anywhere, `find` for `*migration*`/`*.sql` returned nothing). Instead, `src/server/db/migrate.ts` embeds a hand-written `SCHEMA_STATEMENTS: readonly string[]` array of `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ...` / `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS ...` statements, explicitly documented as "kept in step with `src/server/db/schema/**`" and used as a fallback "push" path when `./drizzle` doesn't exist (it never does in this corpus). `runMigrations()` prefers `./drizzle/*.sql` files if present (split on `--> statement-breakpoint`, drizzle-kit's marker), else falls back to `SCHEMA_STATEMENTS`.

**Practical implication for your feature**: to add a new table/column you must (a) add/modify a Drizzle table definition under `src/server/db/schema/*.ts`, (b) re-export it from `src/server/db/schema/index.ts`, and (c) **also hand-add the matching `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS ...` statement to `SCHEMA_STATEMENTS` in `migrate.ts`**, in the same relative position (organizations/members are between `organization_usage` and `projects` in that array). There's no numbered-migration-file convention to follow since none are generated/checked in — just keep the two in sync as the comments there insist.

Members/orgs schema files: `src/server/db/schema/organizations.ts` (`organizations` + `organization_usage` tables) and `src/server/db/schema/members.ts` (`members` + `invitations` tables) — both shown in full above. Shared column helpers in `src/server/db/schema/_shared.ts`: `idColumn()` (ULID text PK), `timestampColumns` (`createdAt`/`updatedAt`, `$defaultFn(nowIso)`), `tenantColumns` (`orgId`), `softDeleteColumns` (`archivedAt`).

## 8. Notification system (`src/types/notification.ts`, `src/server/services/notification-service.ts`)

- `NotificationKind` enum (in `src/types/notification.ts`): `"issue_assigned" | "issue_status_changed" | "issue_due_soon" | "issue_overdue" | "comment_created" | "comment_mention" | "member_invited" | "member_joined" | "project_archived" | "plan_limit_reached" | "digest_ready"`. You'd add e.g. `"ownership_handover_offered" | "ownership_handover_accepted"` etc. here.
- `notify(orgId, kind, recipients: readonly UserId[], payload: {title, body, href, actorId})` is the one write path — it skips the actor themself, resolves per-recipient channel prefs via `preferenceRepo.getPreference` + `resolveChannels`, and no-ops entirely for a recipient whose channels resolve empty.
- **"new member" / owner-targeting pattern** — exactly what you need for "notify the current owner that a handover is pending/accepted":
```ts
subscribe("member.joined", async (payload) => {
  const org = requireFound(await orgRepo.findOrgById(payload.orgId), "Organization", payload.orgId);
  await notify(payload.orgId, "member_joined", [org.ownerId], {
    title: "A new member joined",
    body: `${payload.userId} joined as ${payload.role}`,
    href: "/settings/members",
    actorId: payload.userId,
  });
});
```
It finds "the owner" by reading `org.ownerId` off the denormalized `Organization` row (not by querying members where role=owner) — follow this exact lookup for notifying the current owner of a handover event. This subscription block lives inside `registerFanOut()`, gated by a module-level `registered` boolean and invoked once at the bottom of the file (`registerFanOut();`) — add your new `subscribe(...)` calls inside that same function.

## 9. Existing tests for organization/member services

Files:
- `tests/services/member-service.test.ts` — role changes, last-owner protection, `member.role_changed`/`member.removed` emission (full contents shown above).
- `tests/services/invitation-service.test.ts` — seat quota, rate limit, accept/revoke/expire (full contents shown above).
- No `tests/services/organization-service.test.ts` currently exists (only member/invitation/comment/issue/project/billing/activity/notification/search have service tests) — you'll likely be creating a new test file for handover, or extending organization-service tests for the first time.

Testing pattern (from `tests/server/_support/fixtures.ts` + both test files):
- `vi.mock("@/lib/id", ...)` and `vi.mock("@/lib/logger", ...)` swapped for deterministic doubles (`tests/server/_support/doubles/id.ts` — counter-based ULID-shaped ids; `tests/server/_support/doubles/misc.ts` — fake hash/rate-limit/mentions/logger modules).
- `useTemporaryDatabase()`: creates a real tmp SQLite file, sets `process.env.TASKFLOW_DB_PATH`, runs `runMigrations(path)` — called once in `beforeAll`, cleaned up in `afterAll`. This is a **real** DB through real repositories, not an in-memory fake.
- `createTenant(slug, plan)`: inserts one org + 4 users (one per role: owner/admin/member/viewer) + memberships + a project, returns `{org, project, actors: Record<Role, Actor>, userIds: Record<Role, UserId>}` — `tenant.actors.owner`, `tenant.actors.admin`, etc. are ready-made `Actor`s for tests.
- Assertion pattern for event emission: `subscribe(eventType, payload => resolve(payload))` wrapped in `new Promise`, fire the service call without awaiting inside the promise executor, then `off()` to detach and assert via `toMatchObject`.
- Assertion pattern for permission failures: `.rejects.toBeInstanceOf(PermissionDeniedError)`.
- Assertion pattern for business-rule errors (plain `Error`): `.rejects.toThrow(/at least one owner/)` — regex match on message, since these aren't typed classes.

## 10. Time / clock pattern for 7-day expiry

There is **no injectable/fake clock abstraction** anywhere in the codebase (no `Clock` interface, no `now: () => Date` param threaded through services). The convention instead is:
- Production code calls `new Date()` / `Date.now()` directly at the call site (e.g. `toIsoTimestamp(new Date())` throughout services; `Date.now() + input.expiresInDays * MS_PER_DAY` in `invitation-repository.ts::insertInvitation`).
- Where a function needs to be testable against an arbitrary "as of" time, it takes an explicit `reference: Date = new Date()` **parameter with a default**, e.g. in `src/lib/date.ts`:
```ts
export function formatRelative(value: IsoTimestamp, reference: Date = new Date()): string
export function isOverdue(dueAt: IsoTimestamp | null, reference: Date = new Date()): boolean
export function digestWindow(digestHourUtc: number, reference: Date): {...}
```
- `src/lib/date.ts` also has `addDays(value: IsoTimestamp, days: number): IsoTimestamp` — the exact helper to compute your 7-day expiry: `addDays(now(), 7)` (where `now()` is also exported from `date.ts` as `toIsoTimestamp(new Date())`). Prefer this over hand-rolling `Date.now() + N * MS_PER_DAY` the way `invitation-repository.ts` currently does, since `date.ts` is the intended home for this arithmetic (though note the existing invitation repo doesn't actually use `addDays`/`date.ts` — it inlines its own `MS_PER_DAY` constant, so there's some inconsistency you could either follow or clean up).
- No test I found uses `vi.useFakeTimers()` for these services — the invitation expiry test instead sidesteps real elapsed time by passing a **negative** `expiresInDays: -1` to `insertInvitation` to synthesize an already-expired row (see `invitation-service.test.ts`, "refuses an expired or revoked invitation token" test). Expect to use the same trick (e.g. pass a negative `expiresInDays`/directly construct an already-past `expiresAt`) for testing handover lapse, rather than fake timers.

